[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is Macro, one open-source app that replaces your team\'s entire subscription stack. Email, chat, docs, tasks, calls, file storage, even the CRM. Everything lives in a single fast interface.'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everything is all linked into a knowledge graph, and your team\'s AI agents share one memory across all of it. It\'s built in Rust. It ships a new release basically every single day, and rare words, in 2026, it is fully'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open-source under AGPL. Not open-core with the good parts behind a paywall. There is a catch when it comes to self-hosting this, and the catch is a number, 42. I\'ll explain at the end. Here\'s the velocity check first. 194'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'releases since the code went public in November. The version numbers are literally dates. V2026.8.12 shipped yesterday, and there was one the day before that, and the day before'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that. Over 5,000 commits in nine public months. Whatever you think of everything apps as a category, this particular team is sprinting like the rent is due. And in a category where execution speed is'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything, that\'s the first box checked. Macro is a New York and Toronto startup of about 15 people, and the origin story is refreshingly specific. At their last venture, they ran Slack,'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Linear, Notion, HubSpot, and Superhuman side by side. All good tools, scaled to 20 people, and watched the tool chain dissolve into chaos held together by integrations and Zapier. So, they spent'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two years building one system to run their own company on, dogfooding it daily, and open-sourced it once it could carry the weight. The app is built from blocks, and every block shares one back-end. Email, a real client with a'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unified multi-account inbox and keyboard shortcuts. Messages, channels and DMs built for technical discussion. Tasks, deliberately Linear-inspired. Docs, real-time collaborative, markdown'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'native, built on CRDTs, a canvas for spatial thinking, calls that get recorded, transcribed, and filed straight into team memory, file storage that auto collects every attachment from email and chat. GitHub pull requests'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'linked to tasks, and a CRM with contact enrichment. The connective tissue is the point. Every object can interlink every other object, and those links are stored as a bidirectional graph. And the whole'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thing is genuinely on GitHub. A Rust and SolidJS mono repo with a local Docker Compose stack, so you can clone it and run the full app on your own machine tonight. The repository layout alone is an education. Hexagonal architecture'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'throughout, 42 deployable services, and a style guide more disciplined than most companies ever write down. This line from their readme is the entire thesis, and anyone who has run a team will feel it in their bones. Every team got their'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own tools, and the company was held together by MCP and Zapier. The company was not computable. It was chaotic. Not computable. That is exactly the problem. Your company\'s knowledge'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shattered across five silos that don\'t talk, unreadable to your own people, let alone your agents. So, here\'s the before and after. Before, five subscriptions, five search boxes, five notification streams, context lives in screenshots'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pasted between tools, the Zapier duct tape breaks silently over the weekend, and your AI assistant can see exactly one silo at a time. After Macro, one workspace with one search across email, chat, docs, tasks, and customers, and'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one link graph instead of copy-paste archaeology, and agents with team-level memory that can finally see the whole company at once. One bill instead of five, too, or zero bills if you\'re brave'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enough to self-host. Now, the skepticism you are rightly feeling. Everything apps have a graveyard. Most of them die because they do everything badly. And when they die, your data dies with them.'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Macro has two real answers. First, each block deliberately copies a best-in-class tool instead of reinventing it. Linear for tasks, superhuman for email, so the floor is high.'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Second, and much bigger, it\'s AGPL open source, not open core. If the company vanished tomorrow, the code, your data, and your self-hosted instance would all survive. That\'s the difference between making a'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bet and walking into a trap. Laid out as a grid, the ambition is obvious. Email, messages, tasks, docs, canvas, calls, files, pull requests, CRM, and agents running across all of it. 10 blocks, one'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back end, one graph. This is the Microsoft Office play. Rebuilt for the intelligence age, executed in the open where everyone can watch and and copy the homework. The website pitches it as an office suite'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the intelligence age, and the mobile experience is real. Inbox, channels, and agent conversations from your pocket. With the AI summarizing what the team has been working on while you were out.'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The docs at docs.macro.com cover every block, the migration path from your current stack, and an FAQ that answers the licensing and self-hosting questions with unusual honesty. If you want to test the water without'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'commitment, the hosted free tier plus the public repo makes kicking the tires a zero-risk afternoon. The public numbers, 9 months in, 2,480 stars, 267 forks, over 5,000 minutes, 194 releases.'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Behind that, SOC 2 type 2 and ISO 27001 certification with zero data retention agreements at the model providers. The kind of enterprise homework most'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'startups skip until a deal forces them. And the repository was pushed to today, naturally. And here\'s my favorite chart for judging a project like this. Commits by contributor. Five engineers, each with more than 400 commits, and that\'s'

[05:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just the top of the table. This is not one burned-out founder carrying a dream on nights and weekends. It\'s a funded staff team where the bus factor is an actual bus. For an app you\'d be trusting with your company\'s email, tasks, and customer'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data, that depth matters as much as any feature. The fine print in the starting with that number, 42. That\'s how many deployable services make up Macros back end. Self-hosting is'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fully permitted under the AGPL and genuinely documented. But let\'s be honest about what it is. Yesterday we covered a mail server that\'s one binary. This is a real distributed system. And running it'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yourself is an ops project, not a weekend flex. Realistically, most teams will use the hosted version. Which is a paid product from a venture-backed startup because that is how the open-source gets funded.'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The public repo is only 9 months old. Email support currently means Gmail accounts. And the AI features do talk to model providers. Under zero retention agreement and SOC 2. But map that against your own'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compliance needs. To their credit, every caveat I just listed is answered straight in their own FAQ. So, that\'s Macro. The boldest open-source swing at the work OS problem this channel has covered. And the AGPL license is what'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'turns a risky bet into a hedged one. The repository, website, and docs are linked in the description. Tell us in the comments which of your five work apps you\'d replace first. And which one you\'d'

